e says it will be toppled only  through constitutional means  Hariri maybe hoping his decision to  resign will pressure his coalition  partners to make concessions a Hezbollah  led government needs political cover  from someone like Harry Lee who enjoys  the support of the international  community what began as a spontaneous  protest movement is now a political  crisis that spilled onto the streets  Lebanon's fault lines are deep and it's  unclear what happens next a political  vacuum a precarious situation a divided  Street and economy grinding to a halt  Lebanon has been here before and like in  the past it is the security of the  country which is facing the biggest risk  let's not bring in Zana for an update  from Beirut so what has been the impact  of the high dat resignation on the  streets of Beirut zina  well as you can imagine they're  welcoming this they're calling this a  victory what they have been demanding  release for the government to resign and  for early elections to be held they want  to get rid of a ruling elite that has  been in power for what thirty years now  since the days of the civil war they  believe that they're corrupt they  believe that they've mismanaged the  country so people here are welcoming it  but they're also realizing how dangerous  it is to remain in the streets as you  saw in our package a short while ago  they came under attack by supporters of  the government so now civil society  groups protesters they're talking about  the possibility of opening roads no  longer blocking roads across the country  and instead focus their protest action  outside government institutions and most  likely demonstrate in in some squares  across the country so it is a very  dangerous divide that the Prime Minister  has now resigned he submitted his  resignation to the president the  president has still not spoken or  reacted to that yet we understand he is  going to give his reaction tomorrow on  whether or not he's going to accept that  resignation or whether or not some sort  of a compromise can be reached in order  to avoid a very dangerous political  vacuum in this country but Dana so like  you're saying this is not it for the  protesters because they had not only  demanded Hariri's resignation but a  whole series of other resignations as  well  yes it's a crisis that is far from over  but at the end of the day there their  demands really it's easier it's easier  said than done they're up against a  political elite who controls security  forces even the security agencies in  Lebanon are divided they're affiliated  with the different political parties so  the danger is there those in power  they're clinging on to power the message  has been so far you can't topple us from  the street if you want to topple us you  have to topple us through constitutional  means our constitutional tools because  we won an election in 2018 and it was  legit illegitimate election you see the  problem is those in power now believe  that this is a campaign not just to  improve the conditions and the country  but this campaign has a political agenda  and that is to change the balance of  power in this country because right now  those in power are ally to Iran and some  opposition politicians have been riding  the wave of popular anger and those  politicians are allied with the  pro-western camp so it is a very serious  political crisis we've seen as similar  crises like this in the past Lebanon has  been to the brink on more than one  occasion but they've pulled back but the  question is can a compromise be reached  we're expected to hear from the  Secretary General of Hezbollah on Friday  it will be his third speech really since  the start of this protest movement 13  days ago what is he going to say is he  going to try to calm the streets or is  he going to make an announcement that  will instigate even more anger right now  Hezbollah and and its partners really  have lost a lot of support in the street  especially when their